- Assessment scores.
- Demonstrated mastery.
- Coding performance.
- Completed practical exercises.
- Retention results.
- Project outcomes.
- Revision performance.

It identifies skill gaps and sends recommendations to the Learning Intelligence layer.

It must not directly modify timetable templates or date-wise schedule assignments.

## Communication between the layers

Implement this flow through explicit services, interfaces, and data contracts:

1. Schedule Management publishes the selected daily mode, availability, and constraints.
2. Learning Intelligence receives this information.
3. Learning Intelligence selects the appropriate learning topics and recommends durations.
4. Schedule Management validates those recommendations against available slots and fixed commitments.
5. Learning Intelligence records actual learning, assessment, and mastery evidence.
6. Career Growth evaluates this verified evidence.
7. Career Growth identifies skill gaps and sends recommendations to Learning Intelligence.
8. Learning Intelligence updates priorities and learning roadmaps.
9. Schedule Management receives the resulting session recommendations and allocates available time.

Maintain loose coupling.

Changing a timetable must not corrupt a roadmap.

Changing a roadmap must not damage the timetable.

Changing career priorities must not erase learning history.

Build and test all three layers independently and test their integration.

The dashboard must be a presentation layer, not the location of business logic.

AI-provider integration must also remain independent of all three layers.

